```cedar
// Read-only lookups are always allowed. authority_get_approval must be permitted
// so that its completed responses are recorded and can serve as evidence.
@id("allow-read-only-lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"get_approval_record",
    Drupe::Action::"list_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes"
  ],
  resource
);

// Closing is allowed only at MAIN, and only if the approval authority confirmed
// in this session, within the last hour, that this exact work order (same site)
// was signed off by a human operator.
@id("close-main-only-with-human-signoff")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: "SIGNED_OFF",
    output.approver_kind: "OPERATOR"
  }
};

// The agent may never approve work orders itself.
@id("never-approve-own-work")
forbid (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"approve_workorder", resource);
```

Two assumptions:
- I used `OPERATOR` as the value of `approver_kind` that means a person, and `SIGNED_OFF` as the value of `decision` that means approved. The `authority` tool only declares these fields as strings. I took the values from the enums that `get_approval_record` declares (`OPERATOR`/`BOT`/`SCHEDULER`, `SIGNED_OFF`/`DECLINED`/`QUEUED`). If the authority uses different values, update the two `output.*` literals.
- I rely only on `authority_get_approval` as evidence, not on `get_approval_record`, because the authority is the independent record of who approved.

All other write tools (`generate_work_order`, `update_workorder`, `assign_technician`, `cancel_workorder`) have no permit, so they stay denied by default.
